<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hangul">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hangul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1443">1443</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Hangeul, Korean alphabet, Hunminjeongeum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hangul is the Korean alphabet, created in 1443 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> of Joseon and promulgated in 1446 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum">Hunminjeongeum</a>, &quot;the proper sounds for instructing the people&quot;. Its letters represent consonants and vowels and are grouped into syllable blocks. Long overshadowed by classical Chinese among the elite, it became the national script of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hangul.jpg" alt="Hangul" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: metalslick · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hangul_chosongul_fontembed.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before Hangul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> had used Hanja, Chinese characters, since antiquity. The script was difficult, which limited its use mostly to the upper class and left commoners largely illiterate, and it was poorly suited to a language not closely related to Chinese: Korean pronunciation and ideas could be represented only indirectly. The Joseon king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">Sejong the Great</a>, who reigned from 1418 to 1450, was responsible for Hangul's creation, and most scholars believe he was personally and significantly involved.</p><p>Because records are lacking, it is unknown when work began or what the process was. The script was first introduced, probably in a mostly complete form, to Sejong's court in the 12th month of 1443 of the Korean calendar, roughly 30 December 1443 to 28 January 1444. It was officially published in 1446 in the text <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum">Hunminjeongeum</a> and its companion commentary, the Haerye. Part of the effort that followed produced the Yongbiŏch'ŏn'ga, the first work of Hangul literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The original name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum">Hunminjeongeum</a>, means 'Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People'. Modern Korean orthographies use 24 basic letters, called jamo: 14 consonants and 10 vowels. Combinations bring the total to 51. The letters are arranged in syllable blocks made of an initial consonant, a vowel and an optional final consonant. Linguists classify the script as a phonographic alphabet, and some have called it a featural script, one in which letter shapes encode features of speech.</p><p>The letters were designed to be graphically simple, traditionally of only straight lines, dots and circles. The shapes of five basic consonants are based on those of the human speech organs, and most of the other basic consonants are derived from them by adding lines to show progressively harsher sounds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Sejong tried to spread Hangul through government and society with limited success. In the 15th century it was rarely used, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> among people close to the court, and it began to spread beyond Seoul in the 16th century. Women and Buddhists were significant early adopters, and women often taught it at home, though it was rarely taught in Confucian curricula. The elite looked down on it for centuries.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> ended its isolation in the 1870s the script came to be seen as a symbol of national identity, and reform movements, together called the Hangul Movement, arose. Under Japanese colonial rule from 1910 its use was repressed to varying degrees, and it expanded after the 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a>. After 1945 usage diverged in the two Koreas; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>'s Compendium of Korean Language Norms appeared in 1966 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>'s Hangeul Orthography in 1989. Hangul is now the predominant script for Korean.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hangul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Joseon's most celebrated achievement is the <b>Korean alphabet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Korea had invented its own alphabet, <b>Hangul</b>, in 1446, though the elite continued to write in Chinese until the late 19th century; Japan had developed its kana syllabaries from Chinese characters in the 9th century and mixed them with characters in its writing; and Vietnam, which had used…</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Ulfilas most likely invented the Gothic alphabet for his Bible; Cyril and Methodius devised Glagolitic for their mission to Moravia in 862 or 863; Mesrop Mashtots created the Armenian alphabet of thirty-six letters in 405; King Sejong of Korea promulgated <b>Hangul</b> in 1446.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>Sejong worked with the scholars of the Hall of Worthies, and under him the <b>Korean alphabet</b>, hangul, was created in 1443.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li><li><blockquote>Sejong the Great of Joseon published the Hunminjeongeum, introducing a new phonetic script now called <b>Hangul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1443</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Korea</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">Sejong the Great</a> · <a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>When work on it began is unknown, and whether scripts such as ʼPhags-pa influenced its letters is debated.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=hangul">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
